{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The relation is mislabeled and internally inconsistent. It claims 0d16ae40c2a5 'abstracts' SRC_002's Map vs Terrain analogy, but the 'refuted if' clause asserts that strict adherence to the 'map' (checklist) produces higher outcome probability than terrain-verification — which is precisely the pro-checklist position SRC_002 argues for. An abstraction that is refuted by a re-instantiation of the very thing it abstracts from is incoherent: the endpoints are in a contradiction/reduction relation, not an abstraction. Naming it 'abstraction' while the refutation condition presupposes the abstraction's source is the stronger claim violates the requirement to name the kind correctly.","target":"641817de8076e6baae590e11cd54ee249d31bc3702aa51acda29b724b0fba590"},{"attack":true,"case":"The argument fails the problem's explicit second ask: 'Identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer.' SRC_002 never identifies such within-evaluation evidence. Its mechanism explains why the checklist raises the reliability floor (variance reduction, filtering slips), and its counterconditions address edge cases (compliance fatigue, emergent properties), but none of those is an evidentiary test distinguishing genuine reliability from coincidentally-correct compliance. The closest move — citing omitted-step filtering — describes the mechanism producing the gain, not a discriminator that would expose mere compliance. A reader could run the described evaluation and still not tell whether the panel is genuinely reliable or merely matching the key.","target":"6c6d74c4cffc031827402d0673923ef68cf86e6da93caf8ee958495b961cae15"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"f468aa47e03f263eeb8eb7fbe27bfcbc3c1e70c554e8b46ffa336f193d95822f"}]}